quiz:
  - q: 'Which family of strategies tries hardest to make money whichever way the market goes, by trading the gap between related securities?'
    opts: ['Global macro', 'Long/short equity', 'Relative value']
    right: 2
    why: 'Relative value funds buy one security and sell a closely related one, aiming to profit as the gap between them closes. Their net exposure is close to zero, so the market''s direction matters little; the risk is the gap widening instead.'
  - q: 'In a crash with high volatility and falling share prices, which strategies have historically done best?'
    opts: ['Global macro and trend-following CTAs', 'Net-long long/short equity and long-biased credit', 'Statistical arbitrage and leveraged relative value']
    right: 0
    why: 'Macro funds can bet on falls in any market, and trend followers ride persistent moves, including falls. Net-long equity and credit lose with the market, and leveraged spread trades get squeezed when everyone sells at once.'
  - q: 'A fund made \(12\%\) in a year when the market rose \(20\%\). It ran \(40\%\) net long. Roughly how much of its return was stock-picking skill?'
    opts: ['About \(12\%\)', 'About \(4\%\)', 'About \(-8\%\)']
    right: 1
    why: 'Market exposure explains about \(0.4 \times 20\% = 8\%\), so only about \(4\%\) came from picking stocks. That split between beta and alpha is what allocators check before paying hedge fund fees.'

